\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.3}{\ignorespaces Branching ratios of $\ensuremath  {\text  {A}}\xspace  $ vs. $\ensuremath  {m_{\ensuremath  {\text  {A}}\xspace  }}\xspace  $ for $\ensuremath  {m_{\ensuremath  {\text  {H}^+}\xspace  }}\xspace  = 160$\ensuremath  {\,\text  {Ge\hspace  {-.08em}V}}\xspace  in the four 2HDM types at $\tan \beta = 1$ and 5. The $b\bar  {b}$ channel dominates above $2m_b$; $\mathcal  {B}(\ensuremath  {\text  {A}}\xspace  \to \ensuremath  {\mathrm  {\mu ^+}}\ensuremath  {\mathrm  {\mu ^-}}) \sim 10^{-3}$ is approximately constant. In Type-X, the $\ensuremath  {\tau ^+}\xspace  \ensuremath  {\tau ^-}\xspace  $ channel is enhanced at large $\tan \beta $ due to the lepton-specific coupling structure. The calculation is performed with the 2HDMC\nobreakspace  {}v1.8.0 library.\relax }}{30}{figure.caption.28}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:ADecay}{{2.3}{30}{Branching ratios of $\PA $ vs. $\mA $ for $\mHc = 160$\GeV in the four 2HDM types at $\tan \beta = 1$ and 5. The $b\bar {b}$ channel dominates above $2m_b$; $\mathcal {B}(\PA \to \Pgmp \Pgmm ) \sim 10^{-3}$ is approximately constant. In Type-X, the $\PGtp \PGtm $ channel is enhanced at large $\tan \beta $ due to the lepton-specific coupling structure. The calculation is performed with the 2HDMC~v1.8.0 library.\relax }{figure.caption.28}{}}
